{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba PLAN.md | sed -n '115,250p'\"","aggregated_output":"   115\tUse the full `my-3d2dge.js`, not simultaneous copies of its included modules. Start from the arcade kit’s lifecycle scaffolding, but replace its Galaga-like movement with this game’s marching formation. Use `view:'overhead'`, a fixed combat rectangle, `camera:{zoom:false}` and peripheral expansion only where it preserves that rectangle. Reuse native sprites, animation, renderer, settings and audio. `E.Bullets` may handle projectile storage/drawing, but its hit helpers use endpoint overlap, not swept collision. Implement one swept first-hit resolver for bunker cells, ships, enemies and flagship; do not also apply the helper’s hit damage afterward.\n   116\t\n   117\t**Do not use `E.Arcade.start(2)` as simultaneous co-op.** The arcade helper’s two-player model alternates turns. Use `game.allowJoin({max:2,...})`/player inputs for the local lobby and implement the shared battle state ourselves. Reuse arcade scoring/cards/attract helpers only where their lifecycle matches the design; its default attract delay is 20 seconds and must be changed.\n   118\t\n   119\tSuggested internal organisation, adjustable if the implementation stays coherent:\n   120\t\n   121\t| Relative path | Responsibility |\n   122\t| --- | --- |\n   123\t| `vendor/engine/` | Pinned handoff, necessary documentation, licenses and an explicit engine patch record. |\n   124\t| `vendor/hub/` | Pristine session client and the matching protocol/test assets. |\n   125\t| `src/sim/` | Seeded world state, commands, formation, collision, erosion, salvage, life accounting, flagship and progression. |\n   126\t| `src/content/` | Validated enemy definitions, six-wave/location data, flagship phases, difficulty defaults and balance bounds. |\n   127\t| `src/view/` | Native pixel assets/animation, draw-only presentation and cosmetic effects. |\n   128\t| `src/ui/` | Input action routing, focus, front-end/session overlays, glyphs, dev tools and reports. |\n   129\t| `src/audio/` | Procedural instruments, event sounds and music scheduling. |\n   130\t| `src/session/` | Standalone authority and the actual AI Hub adapter. |\n   131\t| `tools/` | Reproducible build/export and one quiet verification entry point. |\n   132\t| `tests/` | Simulation, browser, input, audio and Hub scenarios. |\n   133\t| `evidence/` | Research provenance, distinct issue log, test summaries and selected visual evidence. |\n   134\t\n   135\tMaintain a compact requirements/acceptance checklist rather than another speculative design document. Do not split trivial code solely to match this table. A clear modular source can still export to one HTML.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe engine updates at 1/120 second and supports `game.advance(seconds)` headlessly. Keep gameplay in an explicit fixed-step simulation with a serializable state: tick, seed/PRNG state, encounter and epoch, formation, entity IDs, ships/deployments, projectiles, bunker masks, pickups, scores and flags. Use a small explicit stateful PRNG or seed plus tracked draw count; native `E.rng` is an opaque closure, not an object with a state accessor. Separate UI, audio nodes, engine objects and cosmetic randomness from that state. Centralise ordering: input, movement, firing, swept collisions, damage/deaths, drops/repairs, progression, then presentation events. Never let draw mutate the world. Use stable collision ordering so a shot cannot both destroy a bunker cell and hit an enemy behind it in the same tick.\n   138\t\n   139\tCreate small adapters around verified APIs, not a replacement general-purpose engine. Rendering consumes snapshots and deduplicated presentation events. Standalone drives simulation from engine fixed updates; Hub authority derives its target tick from the shared Hub clock. Avoid stepping twice through both paths. Seed simulation and visual randomness separately. Save/reset must cover every run-owned field. Engine fades and timer clearing are real lifecycle effects: tests must advance transitions, and code must deliberately reset persistent time scale.\n   140\t\n   141\tContent definitions should contain arrangement, starting height, type distribution, fire schedule, saucer opportunity, salvage rules, backdrop and transition policy. A designer must be able to add a formation or tune a difficulty without editing collision or session code. Validate definitions and give actionable errors. A custom editor is unnecessary; structured content plus the dev preview tools satisfies this need.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 7. AI Hub integration: an architectural requirement\n   144\t\n   145\tResearch verified **Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0** at commit `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`. Read the actual [protocol README](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/README.md), [client](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/session.js) and [shared-world example](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/examples/arena.html). Do not invent a generic room/socket API or defer networking until after the game’s state architecture is fixed.\n   146\t\n   147\tThe final document includes the required `hub:session=1`, `hub:session-mode=shared-world`, `hub:session-players=1-2`, goal and how-to metadata, and an honest round-duration hint. Paste the matching `session.js` unmodified as the **first script immediately after `<meta charset>`**, preserving its banner and keeping it outside bundler transformations. Then load the inlined engine and game. Verify placement and client integrity in the exported artifact.\n   148\t\n   149\tCall `await AIHub.connect({mode:'shared-world',seats:[1,2]})`. A null result selects ordinary standalone flow. A real session bypasses title, attract, local lobby and press-to-start gates. Build state and register handlers, then call `AIHub.ready()` once without waiting for user input or audio unlock. Use the Hub’s supplied start card. Its start event precedes play; begin gameplay only when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n   150\t\n   151\tOnly the host simulates. Guests send bounded action packets and render authoritative state. The host processes its own player input locally because Hub messages do not self-deliver. Each participant controls exactly one roster seat regardless of browser gamepad index; bypass local join handling inside Hub sessions. Derive sender identity from the message callback’s `from` argument, never a packet-supplied player ID. The host must also support `server:true` with no local player: no assumption that a host ship, player input, camera target or audio context exists, and no local input sampling. Use roster IDs and validated seat ownership as network identities.\n   152\t\n   153\tFor this modest shared world, use complete compact snapshots rather than a fragile dependency chain of deltas. Send formation origin/direction and alive/type data, compact bunker masks, active projectiles/pickups, ship state, scores, tick and encounter epoch. Start around 10 Hz state publication and 10 Hz input heartbeats with held actions, repair-edge sequence numbers and acknowledgments; verify actual packet sizes and responsiveness. Consume repeated repair edges once, discard stale packets, bound finite values and reject sender/seat mismatches. Neutralize input after a short stale-input timeout, then recover automatically from a subsequent heartbeat.\n   154\t\n   155\tThe library permits 16 KB per message and 20 sends/second per copy, with burst 40. Unreliable packets around 1,200 bytes or larger fragment, so prefer compact snapshots under that size and reserve capacity for reliable lifecycle events. If the complete designed state exceeds that target, choose a documented reliable/full-state strategy within the verified limits and test it under loss; never truncate the world to fit. These are protocol constraints, not a reason to introduce the performance targets excluded by the brief. Interpolate remote visuals, acknowledge local movement for reconciliation, and never predict authoritative kills, repairs or score awards.\n   156\t\n   157\tCapture `AIHub.rng('world')` once; repeatedly asking for that named generator restarts it. This returns an opaque closure without readable state, so derive one seed from it for the game-owned stateful simulation PRNG and serialize that generator’s state. Synchronize meaningful clocks to Hub time. The engine can discard slow browser frame time, so shared-world progression must not assume rendered frames equal elapsed session time. Keep networking alive while local overlays are open and when there is no local player.\n   158\t\n   159\tHub Options/settings is a local overlay: neutralize that seat’s controls while shared simulation and the Hub clock continue. Suppress native global pause/focus-loss behaviour that would freeze a host. Ignore Options while Create is held, since that chord belongs to the Hub. Disable restart, title, quit navigation and world-changing dev actions for a live session. Use no external sockets, brokers, chat, voice, emotes, custom naming, dialogs or navigation in the session.\n   160\t\n   161\tHandle nonhost departure by clearing that seat’s pending input and ship, continuing with the surviving player, and adjusting only future attack scheduling; do not award reserve lives or points for leaving. A host departure voids the round; there is no host migration. On victory/defeat, the host publishes a reliable terminal snapshot, then calls `AIHub.finish({standings:[activeIds],scores,won})` once; group active co-op teammates as a tie and key scores by roster ID. On `time-up`, finish within the supplied grace period, even mid-encounter; expiry is a team loss unless victory already occurred. Align the recommended round length with the full campaign and label a time-expired result clearly. On every `end` reason, stop input transmission, simulation, networking and audio immediately. Display names as text, never interpreted markup.\n   162\t\n   163\t## 8. Build sequence and review gates\n   164\t\n   165\tComplete each gate before widening content. These are integration checkpoints, not permission requests or stopping points. Independent source research, asset authoring and test design may run in parallel with clear ownership; keep changes inside this project, integrate frequently, and never leave a subsystem as a promised future addition.\n   166\t\n   167\t1. **Verify dependencies and boot.** Read/pin the actual engine and Hub contracts; select the title using the required process. Produce a reproducible one-file export with native engine rendering, title focus, gesture-safe audio and inactive-Hub standalone detection. Smoke-test the exported document from `file://`.\n   168\t2. **Prove the core fight.** Build one fully playable formation with correct march/descent, cannon, swept collisions, mask-based erosion, salvage, repair, deployments, score and genuine win/loss. Give it representative native art and sound so feel can be judged early. Prove an ordinary-rules scripted clear and a deliberate loss.\n   169\t3. **Prove shared authority.** Add simultaneous local play and Hub host/guest/server operation using the same world state. Exercise lossy two-page input, remote repairs, death and terminal results now. A networking problem at this gate must be solved before authoring the rest of the campaign.\n   170\t4. **Complete all playable content.** Author the six formations, three locations and flagship; balance solo/co-op and four difficulties. Every content entry is reachable through both normal progression and dev tools. Play the complete standard run without debug assistance.\n   171\t5. **Finish the product shell.** Complete device-sensitive prompts, native mouse interaction, touch assistance, pause/settings/dev flow, records, results, attract, full About/Credits and feedback. Fill out transitions and animation/audio for every gameplay state. Test session restrictions alongside standalone menus.\n   172\t6. **Verify and ship.** Resolve gameplay, integration and presentation failures; finalize source and feedback data; export the complete artifact and validate that exact artifact. Stop only after the acceptance evidence supports the finished demo.\n   173\t\n   174\t## 9. Verification that earns the completion claim\n   175\t\n   176\tProvide **one command**, for example `node tools/test.mjs`, that orchestrates the project’s checks, prints only failures and a one-line count, and exits nonzero on failure. Use the engine’s headless tools first; browser automation covers actual browser/input/audio/session integration. Capture detailed diagnostics in `evidence/` without flooding normal output. Rerun checks only after changing covered code or final export inputs, not repeatedly for reassurance. This is correctness and usability verification, with no FPS target, benchmark or optimization checklist.\n   177\t\n   178\tUse the engine’s [headless example](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/game/test.mjs), `game.advance`, debug snapshots, synthetic input and `game.errors`. Run draw code through the supported headless stand-in too. Add a small explicit test interface to inspect state and submit ordinary commands; dev cheats must not masquerade as normal input. Browser tests wait for state conditions rather than equating wall-clock sleeps to simulation time.\n   179\t\n   180\tCover these meaningful behaviours:\n   181\t\n   182\t- Seed reproducibility; the last surviving invader’s bounds and acceleration; reversals/descent; breach and collision ordering; missile caps; armour and flagship vulnerability windows.\n   183\t- Friendly/enemy bunker erosion, shots through holes, repair preview/target/cost, full-footprint rebuilding, and no resource use when nothing can be restored.\n   184\t- Deterministic salvage availability, pickup capacity, deployment accounting, simultaneous deaths, protected respawns, the one-time score extra, exhausted teammate behaviour, stage transitions and cleanup.\n   185\t- A complete input-driven solo campaign through all six formations and both flagship phases with normal damage/resources; a complete normal co-op run; losses to both causes. Use an adequately capable bot with observations and ordinary actions, then test repair/saucer/specialist mechanics explicitly if the bot can skip them. No instant kills, invulnerability, hidden difficulty reductions or wave jumps in these completion tests.\n   186\t- Start/pause/settings/dev/reset/replay/focus flows, device/glyph switching, held-button debouncing, gamepad unplug/reclaim, mouse hover/click/aim behaviour, touch movement/fire/repair and cancellation. Use a standard-mapping gamepad stub, not a claimed physical controller.\n   187\t- Attract idle threshold, all wake-input families, consumed wake actions, a repeatable loop, clean next-run state and no persistent score writes.\n   188\t- Browser audio unlock; finite generated samples; expected nonzero signal; muted-bus silence; sensible gain bounds; music’s initial/reset setting at 0.10; no runaway voices. Use `OfflineAudioContext` or an analyser. Signal tests do not prove that a human liked the mix.\n   189\t- Safe handling of unavailable storage, malformed saved data, resizing/orientation, and exported-artifact startup without the development directory.\n   190\t\n   191\tFor Hub integration, vendor the matching [harness](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/harness.html), `bridge.js` and `round.js` together. Serve them and the final packaged game from one local origin. Test supported one-seat and two-seat sessions; use two pages with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, then exercise `scenario=host-leave` and `scenario=server`. Await `body[data-complete=\"true\"]`, require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and fail on page errors or Hub protocol/rate/size warnings. Cover no-gesture readiness, countdown, idle bidirectional heartbeats, stale/out-of-order input, local overlays, time-up, valid results and clean end. Add semantic world-state agreement checks; passing transport checks alone does not demonstrate that remote repairs and deaths agree. The repository’s Node checker imports other helpers, so do not assume copying that one file creates a working test tool.\n   192\t\n   193\tUse the installed Python Playwright 1.63.0 environment described in the CEO brief, or Node Playwright pinned to 1.63.0 if needed. Do not reinstall browsers. Native rendering is Canvas2D; WebGPU lighting is optional and WebGL is not required. Use Chromium for full integration checks and Firefox/WebKit for startup, input and layout smoke checks. Keep one or two browser instances open, close them afterward, and use separate pages/contexts for multiplayer.\n   194\t\n   195\tTake screenshots only for questions assertions cannot answer: combat hierarchy and silhouettes, TV text/focus, portrait touch occlusion, critical telegraph readability, flagship phase distinction and About pagination. Use short native filmstrips when animation timing needs visual inspection. Inspect those images and fix the issues they reveal. A screenshot alone is not a playable-path test. Real controller feel, human listening, real TV distance and physical iOS behaviour remain unverified on this machine and must be reported accordingly.\n   196\t\n   197\tTrack distinct issues found and fixed, with severity and the test that caught them. Count unique problems rather than repeated failing assertions. This supplies honest answers to the required testing feedback; use unknown rather than retroactively inventing a count or observation.\n   198\t\n   199\t## 10. Export, provenance and final acceptance\n   200\t\n   201\tBundle modular development sources into the **single final HTML specified by the builder’s CEO brief**. Prefer an entirely self-contained artifact: inlined full engine, game, pixel assets and synthesized audio, with the pristine Hub client first. No source fetches, audio files or local-module imports at runtime. If a CDN is intentionally used, pin its version and disclose the network dependency. Retain required license notices. Escape literal closing-script sequences in generated inline code, including comments; preserve the upstream Hub client intact and verify its embedding separately.\n   202\t\n   203\tTest the actual exported HTML from `file://` after isolating it from development files, and from the local HTTP origin used by the Hub harness. Verify that it boots, plays, replays, opens credits and renders the full asset set. A development-page pass does not certify the export. Do not place builds or caches in shared temporary storage. For any necessary server bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-chosen port, verify that the served page is this game, retain its PID and stop only that owned process. Inspect no sibling agent folders and kill no unrelated process.\n   204\t\n   205\tPrepare one source-information/feedback data object and render the same material in the in-game About/Credits, the builder’s exact required exit-interview schema and the final chat reply. Include every required source-information field and all testing, engine and prompt feedback questions in their specified order. Use the builder’s actual supplied date/model/environment, actual plan filename, actual versions and actual research/testing. Usage is exactly **added on import**. Never estimate cost, tokens or elapsed usage. Use unknown for unavailable facts and none where the schema explicitly permits it. Catalogue metadata must match the title screen, advertised 1–2-player modes, actual controls and implemented features; use the required allowed genre/type values and tag count. Parse the final JSON and validate its shape.\n   206\t\n   207\tThe planner’s interview is a separate planning deliverable. The builder still writes its own implementation/testing interview based on what actually happened, and must not reuse planner answers as evidence of a completed game.\n   208\t\n   209\tThe build is done when all six formations and the flagship are complete and reachable under normal rules; source-defining movement, erosion, acceleration and finite progression are obvious; repairs add meaningful choice; solo/local/Hub co-op agree on the same game; all input families and lifecycle paths work; attract and dev tools meet their contracts; the exported HTML passes the appropriate checks; and the required reports are present and truthful. No visible TODOs, inert controls, placeholder assets, stubbed sessions, missing phases, or unfinished results. Spend remaining effort improving the weakest part of that complete experience rather than adding an unrelated mode.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
