{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_92","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# Astro Battle Jam\n\n**Cut a lane. Patch the planet.** An original finite planetary-defense shooter inspired by the Astrocade interpretation of Astro Battle / Space Invaders.\n\nOpen the named \"'`.html` in the parent directory in a modern browser. It is the complete, self-contained cartridge, including the full native engine, original pixel assets, synthesized audio, credits and Hub client. There are no runtime downloads. `index.html` is an identical development copy.\n\nA standard run has six authored formations across Jade Coast, Amber Relay and Violet Crown, followed by The Big Rude'\"'s two-phase flagship encounter. Surviving enemies accelerate, both sides erode the four cell-mask bunkers, and every fourth kill drops salvage. Catch it, then rebuild around your current firing corridor. An invader crossing the breach line or exhausted deployments ends the team defense.\n\nMove with A/D or arrows, a mouse target, or the left stick/D-pad. Hold Space/left click/R2/Cross to fire; Q/right click/L1/Square repairs the nearest bunker. Options or Escape opens the local overlay. PlayStation prompts are the default, with Xbox labels in settings. The short control card explains touch movement, clear-lane auto-fire, intentional second-contact firing through cover, and contextual repair.\n\nStandalone co-op shares one world and six total starting deployments. Choose Local Co-op, then join the other seat with Options on a spare standard pad, or 2 on a shared keyboard. Two pads and keyboard/mouse plus a pad are supported. On a shared keyboard, P1 uses A/D, Space and Q; P2 uses arrows, period and slash. Seats lock at deployment and returning controllers reclaim them. AI Hub supplies online sessions; there is no standalone remote room or matchmaking service.\n\nFour difficulties use all six formations and the same flagship. Records are separated by difficulty, solo/co-op and assisted status. The pause menu contains developer encounter/resource tools, seed entry, selected spawns, diagnostics, exact step/time controls and live balance sliders. Changes mark the run assisted. In a live Hub session, mutations are disabled and only local settings/diagnostics remain available.\n\n## Build and verification\n\nNo package installation is needed. The build uses Node's standard library; browser tests use the already installed Python Playwright 1.63.0 environment.\n\n\"'```sh\nnpm run build\nnpm test\n```\n\n`node tools/test.mjs` is the single quiet verification command. It prints failures and one total count, returns nonzero on failure, and writes detailed evidence under `evidence/`. Optional suite arguments are `simulation`, `engine`, `session`, `browser`, and `hub`.\n\nThe simulation suite includes ordinary-action standard solo and co-op campaign clears, both defeat causes, cell erosion, repair corridors, deployment accounting and flagship windows. The engine suite drives the actual exported scripts headlessly, including a complete ordinary campaign, draw paths, menus and attract loops. Browser tests open an isolated copy of the final artifact from `file://`; exercise native input, controller stubs, touch profiles, audio and Firefox/WebKit smoke paths. Hub tests bind an owned loopback server on an OS-chosen port and run the pinned official harness at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, including host departure and server-only authority. Additional semantic tests verify remote repair, death, respawn, overlays and terminal agreement. The server is closed by its owning process.\n\nNormal campaign bots observe the world and return ordinary move/fire/repair commands. They do not edit state, grant resources, jump encounters or disable damage. Developer, attract and isolated collision/network fixtures are reported separately from normal completion evidence. A fast bot clear is not a measurement of a novice human'\"'s first run.\n\nPhysical gamepad feel, human listening, real TV distance and physical iOS behavior are unknown. Phone/tablet and gamepad tests are emulations. Audio assertions prove finite signals, bounded gains and mute silence, not aesthetic quality.\n\n## Source and provenance\n\nSee \"'`evidence/research.md`, `evidence/issues.json`, `vendor/engine/PATCHES.md`, and `src/report.json`. The last object is the single source for the in-game source/feedback pages and the generated root `exit-interview.json`.\n\nOriginal game code and pixel assets were authored for this task. The full engine remains unchanged under its included MIT license. The pristine AI Hub session client is the first script after the character-set declaration, as required by Protocol v1.\nEOF\ncat > evidence/research.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Research and provenance\n\nThe source brief is interpreted as Bally's 1979 Astrocade Space Invaders, subsequently Astro Battle, rather than Sidam's similarly named arcade game. This is the plan's working interpretation, not a new release-history claim. The renamed manual itself bears a 1981 copyright.\n\nExamined sources:\n\n- [Bally Alley HTML manual](https://ballyalley.com/cart_manuals/html_manuals/astro_battle.htm), read in full. It establishes horizontal base movement, upward fire, four difficulty settings, damage to bunkers by both sides, accelerating survivors, lower subsequent formations, a harmless bonus parent ship, row-based 10/20/30/40 scores, 100/200/300 parent bonuses, three starting bases, one extra at 1,000 and an ending after six formations.\n- [Five-page manual scan](https://ballyalley.com/cart_manuals/pdf_manuals/Astro_Battle.pdf), saved as \"'`manual.pdf`. Rasterized and inspected the playing-instructions page, scan page 3. The web screenshot endpoint could not return the scan; local rasterization supplied the visual evidence. Text extraction on these scans is unavailable because the pages are images.\n- [October 1982 comparison article](https://ballyalley.com/articles_and_news/Video_Games_October_1982.pdf), saved as `comparison.pdf`. Inspected scan page 4 / printed page 66. It describes colorful crustacean-like marchers, the unusual marching beat, saucer event and the finite sixth-attack ending. No historical audio listening was performed.\n- [Cartridge-video archive](https://ballyalley.com/astrocade_videos/cartridge_videos/cartridge_videos.html), including its Astro Battle link to [Nice and Games, LSYrGZm0I9Q](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LSYrGZm0I9Q). Metadata identifies a 353-second review published January 29, 2010. Direct MP4 download was denied with HTTP 403. A short HLS clip requested around 80-96 seconds succeeded; see `source-clip.mp4` and `source-filmstrip.png`. Examined eight frames spaced two seconds apart: the cartridge and difficulty menus, horizontal movement of colored four-row troops, alternating silhouettes, a low yellow cannon, four orange arch-shaped bunkers and their visible erosion. The short clip is silent video, so exact historical sound events and audio cadence remain unknown. No ROM or commercial asset extraction was used.\n\nThe game uses original silhouettes and authored four-row/eight-column arrangements; that column count is a new layout, not a claim about the source. Simultaneous/online co-op, deterministic salvage and repair, specialist armour/bombardiers, new locations, the hostile flagship and all writing are original additions. The bonus saucer remains harmless and is visually distinct from the flagship. No fuel/docking loop or diving enemies were imported.\n\nEngine reference: my-3D2dge handoff v0.89.1 at `8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691`. Read README, API card, style/animation/headless guidance, arcade kit lifecycle and native headless example. The arcade helper alternates two-player turns and was not used for simultaneous co-op. Its endpoint bullet helpers were not used for damage.\n\nNetworking reference: AI Hub Protocol v1 / session client 1.4.0 at `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`. Read README, complete client and shared-world arena example. Vendor includes the matching official harness, bridge and round coordinator. Complete world snapshots are approximately a few KB, so they use reliable messages at 10 Hz within the 16 KB / 20 sends per second contract. Inputs are bounded 10 Hz unreliable heartbeats with repair-edge acknowledgment and sequence acknowledgment for movement. Remote presentation interpolates immutable positions; damage, masks, score and results are authoritative.\n\nResearch files are evidence only and are not loaded by the exported game.\nEOF\ncat > vendor/engine/PATCHES.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Engine patch record\n\nThe full \"'`my-3d2dge.js` is unchanged from the v0.89.1 handoff at commit `8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691`. No separate included module or agent-edition copy is loaded with it.\n\nGame-level adapters:\n- Adapt the arcade kit'\"'s native Game lifecycle, fixed overhead camera, sprite/animation, palette, audio and settings facilities.\n- Use one serializable, seeded shared battle simulation for simultaneous co-op; do not use the arcade helper's alternating-turn multiplayer.\n- Resolve swept first hits against the actual bunker cell, ship, enemy or flagship part; do not invoke endpoint damage helpers afterward.\n- Supply concise native-pixel controller-first screens, safe gesture audio unlock, contextual touch controls and local Hub overlays using documented opt-outs (\"'`menu:false`, `autoPause:false`, `pausable:false`). Native settings/set/reset, full-screen and input ownership remain in use.\n- Derive authoritative online ticks from AI Hub time, with a complete reliable snapshot strategy. Engine timeScale remains one; standalone developer time is a run-owned simulation accumulator.\n\nThe build escapes literal closing-script sequences only when embedding engine source in HTML. This does not alter the vendored engine file or executing JavaScript semantics.\nEOF\ncat > vendor/hub/PROVENANCE.md <<'\"'EOF'\nAI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0. All protocol assets vendored unchanged from michaelcrosato/ai-hub commit 35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869. Includes README.md, session.js, bridge.js, round.js, harness.html and examples/arena.html. The repository archive supplied no top-level license file. The session client banner is preserved verbatim and the complete client is embedded as the first script immediately following meta charset. No Hub source patches.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
